Begin Exploring Related Columns
Below is a road‑map of the columns that matter for the requested analysis and the typical filters / join keys you will need.

Relevant columns and their potential usage
• countries.country_name : identify rows where country_name = 'Italy'.  
• countries.country_id   : joins to customers.country_id.  
• customers.cust_id      : main customer key; joins to sales.cust_id.  
• customers.country_id   : link to Italian country_id coming from countries table.  
• sales.cust_id          : link to customers.  
• sales.time_id          : link to times.time_id (the calendar table).  
• sales.prod_id          : link to costs.prod_id (and also to products if product details are ever required).  
• sales.quantity_sold    : needed to compute profit.  
• costs.prod_id / time_id / channel_id / promo_id : join keys that allow you to bring unit_price and unit_cost onto each sales row.  
• costs.unit_price – costs.unit_cost : (unit_price – unit_cost) * quantity_sold = row‑level profit.  
• times.time_id          : actual calendar date; apply WHERE time_id BETWEEN '2021‑12‑01' AND '2021‑12‑31'.  
• Any “bucket” calculation will be done after you aggregate profit per customer for Dec‑2021; once min and max total profit are known you can split (max‑min)/10 to build 10 equal intervals.

Typical conditions you will use
• countries.country_name = 'Italy'  
• times.time_id BETWEEN '2021‑12‑01' AND '2021‑12‑31'  
• After joining sales ↔ costs be sure to match on prod_id, time_id, channel_id, promo_id (the four columns that coexist in both tables).

Exploratory SQL snippets  
The following ten SQLite queries move from very simple inspections to more complex joins; each is limited to 20 rows just to eyeball the data before building the final bucket query you ultimately need.

```sql
-- 1. Description: Italian country rows (confirm country_id for Italy).
SELECT DISTINCT country_id, country_name
FROM countries
WHERE country_name = 'Italy'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Customer IDs that belong to the Italian country_id.
SELECT DISTINCT cust_id
FROM customers
WHERE country_id IN (
      SELECT country_id FROM countries WHERE country_name = 'Italy')
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Verify which December‑2021 dates exist in the times table.
SELECT DISTINCT time_id
FROM times
WHERE time_id BETWEEN '2021-12-01' AND '2021-12-31'
ORDER BY time_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Sample raw sales rows for Italian customers in Dec‑2021.
SELECT s.*
FROM sales AS s
JOIN customers AS c  ON c.cust_id = s.cust_id
JOIN countries  AS co ON co.country_id = c.country_id
WHERE co.country_name = 'Italy'
  AND s.time_id BETWEEN '2021-12-01' AND '2021-12-31'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Bring in unit_price / unit_cost for those sales rows.
SELECT s.prod_id,
       s.time_id,
       s.channel_id,
       s.promo_id,
       s.quantity_sold,
       ct.unit_price,
       ct.unit_cost
FROM   sales  AS s
JOIN   costs  AS ct
       ON  ct.prod_id   = s.prod_id
       AND ct.time_id   = s.time_id
       AND ct.channel_id= s.channel_id
       AND ct.promo_id  = s.promo_id
JOIN   customers AS c  ON c.cust_id = s.cust_id
JOIN   countries AS co ON co.country_id = c.country_id
WHERE  co.country_name = 'Italy'
  AND  s.time_id BETWEEN '2021-12-01' AND '2021-12-31'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Compute row‑level profit for a sample of sales lines.
SELECT s.cust_id,
       s.prod_id,
       s.time_id,
       (s.quantity_sold * (ct.unit_price - ct.unit_cost)) AS row_profit
FROM   sales AS s
JOIN   costs AS ct
       ON  ct.prod_id   = s.prod_id
       AND ct.time_id   = s.time_id
       AND ct.channel_id= s.channel_id
       AND ct.promo_id  = s.promo_id
JOIN   customers AS c  ON c.cust_id = s.cust_id
JOIN   countries AS co ON co.country_id = c.country_id
WHERE  co.country_name = 'Italy'
  AND  s.time_id BETWEEN '2021-12-01' AND '2021-12-31'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Aggregate total December‑2021 profit per Italian customer.
SELECT s.cust_id,
       SUM(s.quantity_sold * (ct.unit_price - ct.unit_cost)) AS total_profit_dec21
FROM   sales AS s
JOIN   costs AS ct
       ON  ct.prod_id   = s.prod_id
       AND ct.time_id   = s.time_id
       AND ct.channel_id= s.channel_id
       AND ct.promo_id  = s.promo_id
JOIN   customers AS c  ON c.cust_id = s.cust_id
JOIN   countries AS co ON co.country_id = c.country_id
WHERE  co.country_name = 'Italy'
  AND  s.time_id BETWEEN '2021-12-01' AND '2021-12-31'
GROUP  BY s.cust_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Find the overall minimum and maximum total profit for Dec‑2021.
SELECT MIN(total_profit_dec21) AS min_profit,
       MAX(total_profit_dec21) AS max_profit
FROM (
      -- reuse query #7 without LIMIT
      SELECT s.cust_id,
             SUM(s.quantity_sold * (ct.unit_price - ct.unit_cost)) AS total_profit_dec21
      FROM   sales AS s
      JOIN   costs AS ct
             ON  ct.prod_id   = s.prod_id
             AND ct.time_id   = s.time_id
             AND ct.channel_id= s.channel_id
             AND ct.promo_id  = s.promo_id
      JOIN   customers AS c  ON c.cust_id = s.cust_id
      JOIN   countries AS co ON co.country_id = c.country_id
      WHERE  co.country_name = 'Italy'
        AND  s.time_id BETWEEN '2021-12-01' AND '2021-12-31'
      GROUP  BY s.cust_id
);
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Place each Italian customer into 10 equal‑width profit buckets.
-- NTILE is not natively available in SQLite < 3.25; here we emulate equal‑width
-- buckets by using (max-min)/10 interval math.
WITH cust_profit AS (
      SELECT s.cust_id,
             SUM(s.quantity_sold * (ct.unit_price - ct.unit_cost)) AS total_profit
      FROM   sales AS s
      JOIN   costs AS ct
             ON  ct.prod_id   = s.prod_id
             AND ct.time_id   = s.time_id
             AND ct.channel_id= s.channel_id
             AND ct.promo_id  = s.promo_id
      JOIN   customers AS c  ON c.cust_id = s.cust_id
      JOIN   countries AS co ON co.country_id = c.country_id
      WHERE  co.country_name = 'Italy'
        AND  s.time_id BETWEEN '2021-12-01' AND '2021-12-31'
      GROUP BY s.cust_id
), stats AS (
      SELECT MIN(total_profit) AS min_p,
             MAX(total_profit) AS max_p
      FROM   cust_profit
), bucketed AS (
      SELECT cp.cust_id,
             cp.total_profit,
             CAST( (cp.total_profit - stats.min_p) /
                   ((stats.max_p - stats.min_p) / 10.0) AS INTEGER) AS bucket_id
      FROM   cust_profit AS cp
      CROSS JOIN stats
)
SELECT bucket_id,
       COUNT(*)                         AS customer_cnt,
       MIN(total_profit)                AS bucket_min_profit,
       MAX(total_profit)                AS bucket_max_profit
FROM   bucketed
GROUP  BY bucket_id
ORDER  BY bucket_id
LIMIT 20;
